(a) Pursuant to the authority of O.C.G.A. §§ 45-9-21 and 45-9-22, the city does hereby adopt a policy establishing the terms and conditions under which it may provide for the defense of officers and employees involved in civil, criminal, or quasi-criminal litigation arising out of the performance of their official duties and under which it may pay part or all of any judgment rendered against any officer or employee of the city, or any monetary settlement of pending litigation against any such officer or employee when any such claim or judgment arises out of the performance of his official duties or is in any way reasonably related thereto and in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) Nothing herein contained shall be construed to constitute any waiver by the city, of the governmental immunity afforded it under the laws of the state; provided, further, nothing herein contained shall be construed in any way to reduce or eliminate the rights of any officer or employee of the city against any other party.
(2) Wherever any civil, criminal, or quasi-criminal litigation is instituted in or before any court of this state or of any other state or of the United States, against any officer or employee of the city, in either such officer's or employee's individual or official capacity, asserting personal liability for damages arising out of the performance of the official duties of such officer or employee or in any way reasonably related thereto, the city, as a part of such officers or employees compensation and terms of employment, subject to the limitations and exclusions as hereinafter set forth in this section, may, in its discretion, undertake to defend said civil, criminal, or quasi-criminal actions on behalf of any such officer or employee.
(3) The city may expend funds for such purposes including, but not limited to, attorney's fees, court costs, deposition costs, witness fees and compensation, and all other like costs, expenses, and fees.
(b) The city shall refuse to undertake to defend said civil, criminal, or quasi-criminal actions brought against any officer or employee if it is determined by the city attorney that:
(1) The act or omission did not arise out of and in the course of the employment of such officer or employee, nor was said act or omission reasonably related thereto;
(2) The officer or employee acted or failed to act because of actual or intentional misconduct, fraud, corruption or malice;
(3) The providing of defense of any such action by the city would create a conflict of interest between the city and the officer or employee;
(4) The litigation involves a criminal offense involving theft, embezzlement, or other like crime with respect to the property or money of or in which said governmental entity has an interest;
(5) Undertaking to defend against such action would not be in the best interest of the city;
(6) The officer or employee acted or failed to act as a result of impaired judgment caused by the voluntary consumption of alcohol or by the voluntary illegal use of any controlled substance as defined by the laws of the state;
(7) Except in an emergency situation or upon an existence of extenuating circumstances, the officer or employee acted, or failed to act, in direct contradiction with the advice of the city attorney.
(c) Where the city has undertaken to defend civil, criminal, or quasi-criminal actions brought against officers or employees as provided by this section, the city, may, in its sole discretion pay all or part of final judgments awarded against officers or employees by courts of competent jurisdiction or any amount payable as a monetary settlement of any such civil action, subject to the limitations and exclusions which follow:
(1) Payments on behalf of officers of employees in satisfaction of any judgment or fine or as a monetary settlement of any pending litigation, shall not exceed the total amount of $5,000.00 for any and all civil actions instituted as the result of any single or continuing incident or occurrence.
(2) No sum shall be paid pursuant to the provisions of this section where punitive or exemplary damages have been assessed against any officer or employee by any court of competent jurisdiction.
(3) No sum shall be paid pursuant to the provisions of this section where the officer or employee has, without prior authorization, been represented by counsel other than the city attorney.
(4) No sum shall be paid pursuant to this section unless the claimant unconditionally releases each and every officer or employee and the city from any and all suits, claims, actions, causes of action, demands, damages, costs, expenses and compensation, on account of or in any way arising out of or related to any such single or continuing incident or occurrence.
(5) Any such disbursement as herein provided shall be deemed to be for public purposes and may be paid from city funds.
(Ord. No. 120, 4-11-1995)

